---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="On Terence Tao's Factorial Diophantine Conjecture — Perqed Journal"
  description="Analysis of the asymptotic leading constant for the Erdős-Graham factorial problem and formalization of 8 theorems in Lean 4."
  activeNav="blog"
>

  <article class="article">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Number Theory · Formal Proving</p>
        <h1 class="article-title">On the Erdős–Graham Factorial Problem: Asymptotics & Lean 4 Formalization</h1>
        <div class="article-meta">
          <time>August 23, 2026</time>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span>Perqed Research Notes</span>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span class="tag">Lean 4</span>
          <span class="tag">Sieve Methods</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="article-content">
        <p class="lead">
          In <em>"Products of consecutive integers with unusual anatomy"</em> (arXiv:2603.27990, March 2026), Terence Tao studied the multiplicative factorial Diophantine equation <code>a₁! a₂! a₃! = m²</code>, establishing near-asymptotics of the form <code>N(x) = x^(1/2 + o(1))</code> and proposing an asymptotic leading term.
        </p>

        <h2>The Problem Setting</h2>
        <p>
          The classical Erdős–Graham question concerns products of consecutive factorials evaluating to an exact integer square:
        </p>
        <pre class="math-block"><code>a₁! a₂! a₃! = m²   (1 ≤ a₁ &lt; a₂ &lt; a₃ ≤ x)</code></pre>
        <p>
          Tao's work established upper and lower bounds on <code>N(x)</code> and highlighted the question of explicit leading constants for the primary solution families.
        </p>

        <h2>The Dominant H=1 Parameter Family</h2>
        <p>
          For solutions with interval gap <code>H = a₃ - a₂ = 1</code>, every solution triple is parameterized by:
        </p>
        <pre class="math-block"><code>(a₁, s(a₁!) n² - 1, s(a₁!) n²)</code></pre>
        <p>
          where <code>s(K)</code> denotes the squarefree kernel of <code>K</code>. Summing across <code>a₁ ≥ 1</code> produces the asymptotic leading constant:
        </p>
        <pre class="math-block"><code>C₁ = ∑ (a₁ = 1 to ∞) 1 / √(s(a₁!)) ≈ 4.265293...</code></pre>

        <h2>Convergence and Lower-Order Terms</h2>
        <p>
          Because <code>s(a₁!)</code> grows exponentially with <code>a₁</code> on average, the series converges rapidly, with the remainder after 50 terms satisfying <code>R(50) &lt; 10⁻⁶</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Higher interval lengths contribute strictly lower-order terms: <code>H=2</code> yields Pell-type families scaling as <code>O(log x)</code>, and <code>H ≥ 3</code> corresponds to elliptic and hyperelliptic curves with finite integer point sets.
        </p>

        <h2>Lean 4 Formalization</h2>
        <p>
          The underlying algebraic structure—including the 3-factorial family for <code>a₁=1</code>, the orthogonal 4-factorial family, and sequence ordering conditions—has been mechanized in pure Lean 4 with zero auxiliary axioms (`sorryAx`-free).
        </p>
        <p>
          The Lean 4 source and numerical verification scripts are available in the <a href="https://github.com/bneb/perqed" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Perqed GitHub repository</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

</BaseLayout>

